 "prompt": "Forecast the price of gold (spot price in US dollars per troy ounce, benchmarked to the LBMA gold price / COMEX spot) as of December 31, 2027.\n\nEstablish the baseline first: the current spot price as of late October 2026, its 2024-2026 trajectory and all-time highs, year-to-date performance, and the current level of official-sector and investor positioning at the start of the forecast window.\n\nResearch and weigh the principal drivers, quantitatively where possible:\n1. Central bank gold purchases (China/PBoC, India, Poland, Turkey, other emerging-market official sector), reserve diversification and de-dollarization trends.\n2. The US Federal Reserve policy path through 2027: real interest rates, rate-cut and rate-hike probabilities, and the historical relationship between real yields and gold.\n3. US dollar strength (DXY, trade-weighted dollar) and its pass-through to gold.\n4. Inflation expectations, recession probabilities, and hedging demand for gold.\n5. Geopolitical and policy risk premia: ongoing wars and conflicts, US and global election calendars through 2027, trade and tariff policy, sanctions regimes.\n6. Investment flows: gold ETF holdings, COMEX futures positioning, retail bar and coin demand in China and India, and the central-bank vs private demand mix.\n7. Supply: mine production trends, recycling supply, and structural supply constraints.\n8. Analyst and market-based forecasts: investment-bank year-end targets for 2026 and 2027, options-implied price distributions, and the gold/silver and gold/copper ratios as cross-checks.\n\nDeliverables: a central forecast with a probable range for end-2027 (for example P10/P50/P90 price levels derived from your research), 3-5 mutually exclusive and exhaustive scenarios with probabilities (bull, base, bear), and at least 10 binary forecasts on price milestones with concrete price levels derived from your research (for example: gold trades above a specific threshold at some point in 2027, or gold ends 2027 below its end-2026 level), each with a probability and resolution criteria anchored to verifiable market data such as LBMA/COMEX prints.",
